From 5869d30d7f36f0e2d7f1946a822af1f8f59cfc94 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <claude@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 12:59:45 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] assembler+linker+vectorizer: consolidate Agent A + Agent B
 (numeric-label diffs, GAS \@ counter, relax/skip fixed point, .set
 local-symbol fix, realmode+crypto encodings, GOTPCREL local-label fix, AVX2
 struct-stride soundness); merged regression suite

Manual consolidation of ms178-1.patch.AgentA.txt and
ms178-1.patch.AgentB.txt against origin/main (495bf49e), done by reading
both diffs in full, cloning origin/main at the exact base commit both
patches were cut from, applying each independently (both apply clean
standalone), and hand-merging the two files where they touch the same
code. Every claim below about what does/doesn't already exist in
origin/main was checked against the actual cloned source, not against
either patch's commit-message narrative.

== Taken from Agent A (unique to A, not present in B at all) ==

* src/backend/x86/assembler/parser.rs: `.set` value-expression fix.
  resolve_set_expr's directive-mnemonic guard treated ANY '.'-leading
  token as a directive and skipped substitution. `.set VALUE` arrives as
  one whitespace-free token, so `.set .Lfound, .Lfound+1` was consumed
  whole as a "mnemonic" and never substituted -- the kernel's
  extable_type_reg register scan (.irp + .ifc counting into .Lfound)
  failed on every uaccess object. Fixed by distinguishing directive
  mnemonics (pure `.identifier`, not `.L`-prefixed) from expressions.
  Verified by hand: for the token ".Lfound+1", the fixed is_mnemonic
  check already fails on the '+' before the .L exclusion even applies;
  the .L exclusion is what's needed for the bare ".Lfound" case with
  no operator. Kept A's four unit tests for this area.
* src/backend/x86/assembler/parser.rs: directive-vs-macro guard
  (`!macro_name.starts_with('.')`) so a macro named like a directive
  word can't capture a directive line. NOTE (found during this audit,
  not claimed by either patch): given how `macro_name_candidate` is
  actually derived a few lines above (plain whitespace-split, no dot
  stripping), a directive line's candidate already includes its leading
  dot and a same-named macro's HashMap key never does, so the exact
  map-key comparison already discriminates them without this guard for
  every case exercised by the included test. Kept anyway as cheap,
  correctly-reasoned defense-in-depth documenting the invariant; flagged
  here so it isn't mistaken for the fix that mattered (resolve_set_expr
  is the one with real bite).
* src/backend/i686/assembler/elf_writer.rs, encoder/mod.rs (i686):
  R_386_16 for `.word sym` (was always emitted as R_386_32, corrupting
  the following field and rejected by the kernel's relocs tool against
  realmode segment symbols); pusha/popa/pushaw/popaw for
  arch/x86/boot/bioscall.S.
* src/backend/x86/assembler/encoder/{avx,mod,registers}.rs: vpblendvb
  with a memory rm operand (VEX /is4, crc-pclmul-template.S), suffixless
  `crc32` sized from its source operand, monitorx/mwaitx (AMD, 0F 01
  FA/FB). Spot-checked all three against known encodings: R_386_16 = 20
  matches the i386 psABI; PUSHA/POPA/PUSHAW/POPAW opcodes are the
  standard 60/61/66 60/66 61 forms; vpblendvb's AT&T operand order
  (mask, rm, vvvv, dst) and is4-byte placement `(mask << 4)` match the
  VEX.is4 3-source-operand convention; MONITORX/MWAITX 0F 01 FA/FB match
  published AMD encodings. crc32's size-from-source-operand inference is
  correct per the ISA (dest width and REX.W follow the source r/m width
  in every defined CRC32 form).
* src/backend/x86/linker/emit_exec.rs: GOTPCREL against a symbol with no
  GOT slot (a local asm label reached via `sym@GOTPCREL`) relaxes
  mov->lea like GNU ld instead of silently loading the symbol's own
  first bytes as a pointer; any other opcode shape fails loudly instead
  of emitting silently-corrupt code.
* GAS `\@` macro-invocation counter (global AtomicU64). Functionally
  identical to Agent B's independent implementation of the same fix;
  A's version kept since it was already integrated with the directive
  guard above in the same function.

== Taken from Agent B (unique to B, not present in A at all) ==

* src/backend/elf_writer_common.rs: numeric-label symbol diffs. A GAS
  numeric local label (`770b`, `1f`) has no ELF symbol, so a diff
  involving one can never be resolved as a named-symbol relocation --
  `.long 770b + 1 - 760b` (the kernel's la57 trampoline, locating the
  immediate field inside a far jump) silently stayed zero. Now routed
  through the existing deferred/positional path regardless of size (the
  prior `size <= 2` deferral only covered byte/word diffs; la57's is a
  4-byte `.long`), resolved via the existing same-section
  `resolve_numeric_label` helper with a new cross-section fallback
  (`.pushsection .data` referencing labels still in `.text`) that
  `resolve_numeric_label` intentionally doesn't handle (it requires
  `sec_idx` equality by design). Confirmed against the actual base
  source that `resolve_numeric_label` and `numeric_label_positions`
  already existed and that the fallback adds real, previously-absent
  capability rather than duplicating it.
* src/passes/vectorize.rs: AVX2 reduction-vectorization soundness fix.
  `transform_reduction_avx2`'s element-index scheme scales a GEP's byte
  offset by the vector width, which is correct only when the offset is
  literally `index * elem_size` (contiguous elements). An
  array-of-structs access (`sum += arr[i].i` over `struct S{int i,j,k;}`)
  steps `sizeof(struct)` = 12 instead of `elem_size` = 4, so 8 vector
  lanes are not 8 consecutive `.i` fields; the unguarded transform
  produced `i * 12 * 8` and silently returned 6 instead of 12 for a
  3-element array. Added `offset_is_index_times` and a precondition
  check that bails out (scalar fallback) unless the offset is provably
  `index * elem_size` via `shl`-by-log2 or `mul`-by-constant. This is a
  genuine miscompilation fix with no equivalent in Agent A's patch.
* src/driver/cli.rs: flag-handling regression tests only (mno-* ISA
  flags accepted, unimplemented enabling flags rejected, stack-boundary
  bounds, stack-protector/mcount hardening flags refused rather than
  silently ignored, -mskip-rax-setup). No corresponding logic change is
  present in either patch, which means these tests assert EXISTING
  driver behavior as a regression lock rather than fixing new bugs --
  could not confirm they currently pass without a build (see
  Validation, below); if any fails, the fix belongs in the flag-parsing
  table in src/driver/cli.rs, not in this test file.
* tests/regression/aggregate_dse_soundness.c: five aggregate
  dead-store-elimination miscompile shapes (read-set blind to
  intrinsic args, variable GEP offsets collapsed to 0, untracked
  pointer PHIs, a strict self-referential-PHI rejection rule, an idom
  usize::MAX sentinel indexing panic). No corresponding fix is present
  in B's diff to src/passes/aggregate_copy_forward.rs either. CHECKED
  against the actual current source: unknown/variable GEP offsets,
  pointer-PHI tracking, and an explicit `if parent == usize::MAX {
  return false }` idom guard are already present on origin/main. This
  reads as a regression-lock test for bugs already fixed upstream, not
  a dangling reference to unfixed ones -- but, as with cli.rs, actually
  running it is the only real confirmation.
* tests/regression/kernel_altinstr_layout.c,
  tests/regression/kernel_asm_macro_semantics.c,
  tests/regression/kernel_flags_and_builtins.c: broader regression
  coverage than A's equivalent tests for the same underlying fixes,
  plus real net-new coverage neither patch tests elsewhere: TAB-
  separated instruction prefixes (`rep\tstosl`), segment-prefix
  mnemonics (`ds nop`), macro invocations spliced in through a quoted
  argument after a `;`, C23 `typeof_unqual` (incl. through a macro on a
  const-qualified lvalue), `__has_attribute`/`__has_builtin` in CODE
  position (not just `#if`), fortify-string builtins, and the
  variadic-vs-non-variadic %al/SSE-count calling-convention contract.

== Where A and B overlap (same idea, different code; one kept) ==

* emit_elf `.skip`-vs-jump-relaxation ordering. Both patches insert an
  extra relax_jumps() + fixup_alignment_markers() pass before the
  existing resolve_deferred_skips(), reaching the same GAS-matching
  smallest fixed point, for the same reason (retpoline.S
  FILL_RETURN_BUFFER over-pads if skips are sized against un-relaxed
  jumps; sev.o's jmp-over-two-paddings needs the *second*,
  already-existing relax pass to stay correct). They differ in ONE
  concrete way: A gates the new pass on `A::supports_deferred_skips()`;
  B runs it unconditionally, matching the (already-unconditional)
  second relax pass a few lines later. CHECKED against the actual
  trait: `X86Arch` has exactly two implementors (I686Arch, X86_64Arch)
  and both override `supports_deferred_skips()` to `true`, so the
  `false` default is currently unreachable and the two placements are
  behaviorally identical today. Took B's unconditional placement as the
  more defensible one going forward (consistent with the second pass,
  doesn't silently stop protecting a hypothetical future arch that
  returns `false`), on top of A's file otherwise.
* GAS `\@` pseudo-variable (global `AtomicU64` invocation counter).
  Independently-written, functionally identical implementations. Kept
  A's (already sitting next to the directive guard in the same
  function); B's is equivalent in every observable way, differing only
  in whether the counter is converted to a String before or after the
  fetch_add.
* Both patches' commit messages narrate auditing "the other agent's
  revision," including A's claim to have "repaired a latent bug B's
  directive guard introduced." The B revision actually supplied here
  contains neither the directive-vs-macro guard nor any resolve_set_expr
  change at all (verified by grep over the full B diff), so that
  specific narrative doesn't match the artifact reviewed in this
  session -- possibly describing an earlier/different B revision not
  preserved as a separate file. Noted for provenance; it doesn't change
  any technical conclusion above, since every functional claim here was
  re-derived from the actual diffs and the actual cloned base source
  rather than from either commit message.

== Test suite: union of both patches' new files (17 files = 15 .c + 2 .flags,
   no name or symbol collisions -- each is an independent standalone `main()`) ==

